    description: "Every consumer of supply_chain_tier must agree that tier-2 (internal_dep) is PROJECT-SIDE / project-adjacent code, not an external-dependency sink. Tier-axis concept audit (2026-07-15) found the value is read two contradictory ways: metrics.py:122-141 comments \"External-dependency tiers (2/3) are graph SINKS ... reads ~0\" and buckets tier-2 with tier-3 as external; while ranking.py:162 (weight 1.5, adjacent to first-party 2.0), cfg.py:1655 (DDG default max_tier=2 includes tier-2 as an analysis target), entrypoints.py:1622 (vendor penalty only at tier>=3, so tier-2 escapes it), and sketch.py:5747 (keeps tier-2) all treat tier-2 as project-priority code. A single tier-2 node (config internal_package_roots, a workspace-sibling dependency declaration per PR #655, or a synthetic linker node) is therefore simultaneously up-weighted as project code AND described as an external sink. Root cause: metrics.py inherited the pre-ADR-0041 \"tier-2 = declared direct external dependency\" meaning that ADR-0041 §1 retired. Apex/peer overloading (the most diagnostic leakage test): same value, two roles across consumers.\n\nCross-ref: WI-modom (done) fixed the by_supply_chain_tier.edges=0 COUNT but left the external-sink FRAMING. FIX: reconcile metrics.py to treat internal_dep as project-side (distinct from external_dep); add a consistency guard/test so a new tier consumer can't reintroduce the split. Evidence + verdict in ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-supply_chain_tier_07152026.md (F2)."  # 7202
    fields:  # 7202
      statement: "All supply_chain_tier consumers must treat tier-2 internal_dep as project-side code, not an external-dependency sink; metrics.py currently buckets it with external_dep while ranking/DDG/entrypoints/sketch treat it as project-priority code."  # 7202
      root_cause: "metrics.py inherited the pre-ADR-0041 'tier-2 = declared direct external dependency' meaning that ADR-0041 §1 retired; no consistency guard binds the tier consumers to a single internal_dep semantics, so they drifted apart."  # 7202
